          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"saint-exupery-le-petit-prince-30c49e98e5efaa7b\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"The flower directly avows that she loves the little prince and explains that her previous conduct prevented him from knowing it. The words disclose a loving state rather than undertake a new relational commitment. Their calm, deliberate placement within an apology and explanation provides no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The farewell context makes the avowal an enactment of tenderness in a broad sense, but the core words neither promise continued commitment nor appear reflexively produced, so this does not materially support P or E.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"The flower addresses the little prince directly in represented dialogue: « Mais oui, je t'aime ».\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"At the moment of the little prince’s departure, the proud flower abandons her customary reproaches, apologizes, and explicitly reveals affection that her evasions had concealed. « Mais oui » gives the avowal a corrective force: he failed to recognize her love because she communicated it badly. Her subsequent concern for his happiness and concealment of her tears support sincerity and tenderness, while her composed explanation weighs against treating the words as a reflex-like emotional outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute. »\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense avowal and the claim that he did not know it strongly support truth-conditional disclosure of an existing loving state.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, the flower says that she has been foolish, asks forgiveness, and tells him to try to be happy; the prince perceives « cette douceur calme ».\",\"supports\":\"The orderly apology and calm gentleness frame the utterance as a considered avowal, not an expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After the avowal, she accepts his departure, rejects the protective globe, tells him to leave, and hides her tears out of pride.\",\"supports\":\"Her conduct contextualizes the avowal as sincere and self-effacing, but it does not make the core words a promise or relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier narration says that her tenderness lay behind her « pauvres ruses » and that the little prince had failed to judge her by her actions.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal explicitly reveals a pre-existing affection that had previously been obscured, reinforcing T rather than P or E.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the utterance as a direct avowal of concealed love. Its farewell tenderness, pride, and self-effacement are contextual features adequately described without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
